  "hints": {
    "perField": {
      "zscore": "<strong>Formula:</strong> z = (x − μ) / σ<br><br><strong>Steps:</strong><br>1. Subtract the mean from the value: (x − μ)<br>2. Divide by the standard deviation<br><br><strong>Interpretation:</strong> The z-score tells you how many standard deviations the value is from the mean. Positive = above mean, Negative = below mean.",
      "x": "<strong>Formula:</strong> x = μ + z · σ<br><br><strong>Steps:</strong><br>1. Multiply the z-score by the standard deviation<br>2. Add the mean<br><br><strong>Think of it as:</strong> Start at the mean, then move z standard deviations away.",
      "z1": "<strong>Formula:</strong> z = (x − μ) / σ<br><br>Calculate the z-score for distribution A using its mean and standard deviation.",
      "z2": "<strong>Formula:</strong> z = (x − μ) / σ<br><br>Calculate the z-score for distribution B using its mean and standard deviation.",
      "comparison": "<strong>Comparing z-scores:</strong><br><br>The more extreme value has the z-score with the larger <em>absolute value</em>.<br><br>• |z| = 2 is more extreme than |z| = 1<br>• z = -2.5 and z = 2.5 are equally extreme<br><br>Compare |z₁| vs |z₂| to determine which is more extreme.",
      "b": "<strong>Formula:</strong> b = r · (sᵧ / sₓ)<br><br><strong>Steps:</strong><br>1. First, divide sᵧ by sₓ to get the ratio<br>2. Then multiply that ratio by r<br><br><strong>Tip:</strong> The sign of b always matches the sign of r!",
      "a": "<strong>Formula:</strong> a = ȳ − b · x̄<br><br><strong>Steps:</strong><br>1. First, multiply b by x̄<br>2. Then subtract that product from ȳ<br><br><strong>Watch out:</strong> If b is negative, subtracting a negative means you add!",
      "mean": "<strong>Formula:</strong> x̄ = Σxᵢ / n<br><br><strong>Steps:</strong><br>1. Add up all the values<br>2. Divide by the count (n)<br><br><strong>Tip:</strong> The mean is the \"balance point\" of the data.",
      "deviations": "<strong>Deviation:</strong> Each deviation is (value − mean)<br><br>For each value in the data, subtract the mean. Keep the signs!<br><br><strong>Example:</strong> If data is [2, 5, 8] and mean is 5:<br>• 2 − 5 = <strong>−3</strong><br>• 5 − 5 = <strong>0</strong><br>• 8 − 5 = <strong>3</strong><br><br>Enter as: −3, 0, 3",
      "deviationsSquared": "<strong>Squared deviations:</strong> Square each deviation<br><br>Take each deviation and multiply it by itself.<br><br><strong>Remember:</strong> Squaring a negative gives a positive!<br>• (−3)² = 9<br>• (0)² = 0<br>• (3)² = 9<br><br>Enter as: 9, 0, 9",
      "sumSquaredDev": "<strong>Sum of squared deviations:</strong> Σ(xᵢ − x̄)²<br><br>Add up all the squared deviations from the previous step.<br><br><strong>Example:</strong> 9 + 0 + 9 = 18",
      "df": "<strong>Degrees of freedom:</strong> df = n − 1<br><br>For a <em>sample</em> standard deviation, we divide by (n − 1), not n.<br><br><strong>Why?</strong> This corrects for the fact that we estimated the mean from the same data.",
      "variance": "<strong>Variance:</strong> s² = Σ(xᵢ − x̄)² / (n − 1)<br><br>Divide the sum of squared deviations by the degrees of freedom.<br><br><strong>Example:</strong> 18 / 2 = 9",
      "stdDev": "<strong>Standard deviation:</strong> s = √s² = √variance<br><br>Take the square root of the variance.<br><br><strong>Example:</strong> √9 = 3<br><br><strong>Accepted formats:</strong> You can enter:<br>• A decimal: <code>2.83</code><br>• Square root: <code>sqrt(8)</code> or <code>√8</code><br>• Expression: <code>2*sqrt(2)</code> or <code>2√2</code>",
      "slopeSign": "<strong>Key relationship:</strong><br><br>The sign of slope b <em>always</em> matches the sign of r.<br><br>Think about it: sₓ and sᵧ are always positive (they're standard deviations), so the only thing that determines the sign of b is r!",
      "insight": "<strong>The ratio sᵧ/sₓ is key:</strong><br><br>b = r × (sᵧ / sₓ)<br><br>• If sᵧ = sₓ → ratio = 1 → b = r<br>• If sᵧ = 2sₓ → ratio = 2 → b = 2r<br>• If sᵧ = sₓ/2 → ratio = 0.5 → b = r/2<br>• If sᵧ = 3sₓ → ratio = 3 → b = 3r<br><br>The slope is <em>r times the ratio</em>!"
    },
